Subject: JSON.stringify keeps each cached shape's key fragments side by side

What this does
  - `JSONKeyFrags` / `json_key_frags`: when a shape enters walk_ok with plain keys, the `"key":` bytes of all of them are laid out in one block with offsets, plus 16 spare bytes
  - the shape walk's fast write copies the fragment from that block (a fixed 16-byte copy when it fits) instead of reading each key's atom string
  - the block is counted (`ref`): the walk_ok slot holds it and so does each walk using it, since a nested object can replace the slot during the walk

Why we need it
  After 0594 the key write read each key's atom string header and bytes, scattered across the heap. json_app stringify only: -7.1% cycles, -3.1% instructions.

Soundness
  The bytes are those 0594 wrote for the same plain keys of the same held, hashed shape. The fixed copy writes at most 16 bytes past the fragment into the buffer's spare room (checked: fragment length + 17 bytes free) and reads only within the block's 16 spare bytes; b->len advances by the fragment's length. A walk takes a count on the block before it starts and drops it on both exits, so a slot replacement by a nested object frees it only after the walk. Differentials identical old vs new: new jkey2.js (6,999 outputs, objects nested 3 deep from 60 key sets so walk_ok slots are replaced during walks), jkey, jstr, jprim, jedge, jdiff; jkey2 (600 iterations) under valgrind: no invalid reads or writes, nothing lost (only the conservative stack scan's known uninitialised reads).

